window.TASK1 = [{"date":"1月6日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe table below shows the production of milk annually in four countries in 1990, 2000 and 2010.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The table shows the amount of milk that was produced divided into 4 countries -Netherlands, Australia, Tanzania and Guatemala in three separate years, 1990, 2000 and 2010.\n\nOverall, there are two key points to note. First, throughout the entire 20 year period, the amount of milk produced by Netherlands outnumbered/was much greater than other three countries. And secondly, all countries were similar in that they all increased their milk production by varying amounts during the same period.\n\nLooking at the table in more detail, we can see that there was a moderate increase in the amount of milk production in Netherlands from 1,123,600 litres in 1990 to 1,126,000 litres in 2010. This amounts to a slight increase of only 2400 litres in a 20 year period. This also means that the figure for 2010 was much greater than Tanzania and Guatemala combined.\n\nTurning to the country of Australia, it is noteworthy that at the beginning of the period in 1990, the figure for milk production amounted to slightly just under 679,000 litres. However, after peaking at more than 970,000 litres in 2000, the figure had dropped reasonably to slightly fewer than 800,000 litres. This represents a reasonable increase of approximately 121,000 litres between 1990 and 2010.","words":207},{"date":"1月13日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe table below shows the fishing industry in a European country according to four indicators in 2022.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The table presents information of large-scale industrial ships and small-scale fishing boats in a country in Europe for the year 2002.\n\nIn general, although more people were employed in small-scale fishing boats, smaller vessels had less catch than large ships. Besides, the operating cost of larger ships far outnumbered that of small boats both in terms of annual pay for employees and the fuel consumption.\n\nLooking at the information in more detail, we can see that only 500,000 people worked in big-scale industrial ships, and this figure was significantly less than the number of those working in smaller fishing boats.\n\nHowever, industrial ships noticeably outnumbered fishing boats in terms of annual catch. The former caught 29 million tonnes of fish for food, but only 24 million tonnes of fish was caught by small fishing boats for this purpose. Also, while the quantity of fish caught for industrial process by large ships was 22 million tonnes, small fishing boats showed no records of catch in this item.","words":166},{"date":"2月3日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe chart shows the proportion of renewable energy in total energy supply in 4 countries from 1997 to 2010.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The bar chart illustrates the extent to which four countries relied on renewable energy as a proportion of their total energy in 1997, 2000 and 2010.\n\nOverall, Iceland and Sweden demonstrated a greater reliance on clean energy, whereas Turkey and Australia showed a contrasting trend.\n\nIn 1997, Iceland stood out as the country with the highest proportion of renewable energy usage, surpassing 45% of its total energy supply. Meanwhile, this figure was nearly 7% higher than that of Turkey. Iceland ’ s reliance on renewable energy rose\n\nsignificantly to 60% in 2000, followed by a further rise, reaching 70% in 2010.\n\nConversely, a reversed pattern was seen in Turkey, with a slight rise and a noticeable fall to just over 30% in 2010.\n\nIn contrast, Australia and Sweden relied less heavily on renewable energy. Starting at just below 10%, Australia’s renewable energy usage had halved to 5% by the end of this period. On the other hand, there was a notable increase in Sweden, though it remained below 10%.","words":171},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe charts and table below show the percentage of customers choosing different kinds of websites when shopping online and the percentage of customers shopping online for different reasons in two countries in 2020.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The pie charts compare the preference of websites for online shopping among consumers in Australia and New Zealand, and the table reveals motivations that moved consumers to shop online in these two countries in 2020.\n\nObviously, more people chose to shop online both through foreign and local websites in these two countries. Besides, a lower price was the dominant factor for buyers to make a purchase online in both countries.\n\nLooking at the pie charts in more detail, we can see that customers shopping online via both international and domestic websites accounted for the largest proportion among all the surveyed people, with the figure of 64% in Australia and 50% in New Zealand. By contrast, only 16% of Australian buyers and 20% of those in New Zealand relied solely on local websites. The rest online shoppers in these two countries were attracted by international websites.\n\nIn terms of main reasons for shopping online,it is clear that more than half of the surveyed consumers in both countries decided to make a purchase because of lower prices. Better range of products and convenience were considered by roughly the same percentage of Australian customers, with the figure of around 20%. However, in New Zealand, the proportion of people buying items due to their range of choices was\n\n34%, which was almost 3 times as large as the figure for convenience.","words":227},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe line graph and pie chart below show the number of three groups of immigrants from 2000 to 2008 and the percentage of immigrants for different reasons\nin 2008.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The given charts provide data on the intended length of stay for immigrants to the UK from 2000 to 2008 and the reasons for migration in the year 2008.\n\nFrom 2000 to 2008, there was a clear trend in the length of time migrants intended to stay in the UK. Migrants intending to stay for 2 years or more were the largest group, with numbers fluctuating around 250,000, but generally on an upward trend. The line for migrants intending to stay in the UK for 2 to 4 years was rather flat, fluctuating around 150,000. In contrast, the group of migrants planning to stay for less than 2-4 years was the smallest, growing from 70,000 in 2000 to around 100,000 in 2008.\n\nIn terms of the reasons for migration in 2008, work was the predominant reason, accounting for 38% of immigrants. This was followed by study purposes, which constituted 32%. Accompanying or joining family was the reason for 16% of the immigrants, while 7% did not state a reason, and another 7% migrated for other reasons.\n\nOverall, the majority of immigrants intended to stay for a longer period, with work and study being the main reasons for moving to the UK in 2008. The data indicates that the UK has been a consistent draw for those seeking employment and education, reflecting the country's economic and educational appeal to foreigners.","words":229},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe table compares the proportion of water usage for domestic industrial and agricultural purposes in six countries of the world in the year 2003.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The table presents information about residential, industrial and agricultural water usage in six different countries for the year 2003.\n\nIt is clear that in Canada, Russia and UK, more water was distributed in industry, while agricultural water usage accounted for the largest part in China and Turkey. Australia was the only country where people used most of the water for their daily life.\n\nLooking at the information in more detail, we can find that Canadians, Russians and British people used most of their water for industrial process, with the figure in Canada exceeding 80%. Also, water used for the other two purposes shared a similar figure in the former two countries, with the figure of roughly 10% and 19% respectively. However, the proportion of domestic water usage in Britain(23%) was around 5 times as large as the figure for agricultural water usage in this country.\n\nThe figures in the other three countries showed a different pattern. The majority of water resources was allocated to agriculture in China and Turkey, with the figure of 87% and 73%. In both of these two countries, a similar figure could be found in the water used for daily life and industry. In Australia, however, 65% of the water went to domestic use, but water consumption in industrial field represented only 2%.","words":215},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe difference of women's and men's time spent on leisure activities and household activities\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"Demonstrated in the chart and the table is the time spent on relaxation and domestic work by males and females in 2008.\n\nAs is shown in the table, both men and women spent the overwhelming majority of their time on TV, video, and radio, with men 137 minutes per day while women 118 minutes. Men tended to read for 18 minutes per day whereas women 19 minutes. Compared to men, women were less willing to do sport.\n\nIt is manifest from the chart that on an average day, women were more likely to undertake household chores than men. Specifically, it took women and men about 76 minutes and 30 minutes to cook and wash, respectively. Shopping took up 30 minutes of a woman per day whilst 22 minutes of a man. The situation also applied to the clothes washing and ironing group. By contrast, repair was the only group which cost more time of a man than a woman per day.\n\nIn general, both the chart and the table showed that apart from two very special groups women spend more time in both leisure and household activities than men per day.","words":191},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe charts below give information on the ages of people who got married in one particular country in 1998 and 2008.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The bar charts compare males and females in terms of the age of getting married in one country for the year 1996 and 2008.\n\nIt is clear that in both years, the majority of men and women chose to get married between 26 and 35.\n\nIn 1998, only 1% of women got married between 16 and 19, and there were no changes to this figure until 2008. However, no men chose to start a family at this age group in these two years. In both years, more females than males decided to enter into a marriage between the ages of 20 and 30. Especially, the largest proportions of both men and women decided to get married between 26 and 30 in the initial year, with the figure of 13% and 15% respectively. Subsequently, a marginal decline by 2% could be found in these two figures.\n\nIn terms of the rest age groups, we can see that originally, the same percentage of men and women decided to get married between the ages of 31 and 35(13%). Afterwards, the gender gap expanded as the figure for men witnessed a dramatic growth to 14%, while the proportion of women declined slightly by 3%. In 1998, the percentage of men aged 35-40(11%) was slightly larger than the figure for women. Then, in 2008, the figures for both genders experienced a noticeable drop but the percentage of\n\nfemales overtook their male counterparts.","words":235},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe table below gives information about the proportion of women as workforce and the proportion of women as managers in five different countries.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"Country\n\nWomen as % of workforce\n\nWomen as % of managers\n\nAustralia\n\n42\n\n43\n\nEgypt\n\n20\n\n12\n\nSri Lanka\n\n42\n\n9\n\nJapan\n\n48\n\n17\n\nUnited States\n\n46\n\n43\n\nThe table illustrates the percentages of female workers and female managers in Australia, Egypt, Sri Lanka, Japan, and the United States.\n\nAs for the proportion of female front-line workers in those countries, it is the largest at 48% in Japan, 2% higher than in the United States. Notably, Australia and Sri Lanka have the same proportion of women in labour force at 42%. In contrast, the percentage of female Egyptians who are workers is less than half of those in the other four countries, being noted at only 20%.\n\nMeanwhile, Australia and the United States have fewer disparities between female front-line employees and managers, and the percentages of Australian and American women in administrating roles are same and by far the highest among those in the given countries (43%). However, only Australia has fewer women managers than women workers. On the other hand, the rates of female managers and female workers in the remaining three countries have significant gaps. Specifically, in Sri Lanka, the former is only 9%, nearly five times less than the latter, whereas in Egypt and Japan the figures stand at 12% and 17%, respectively.\n\nOverall, Australia is the only country where the percentage of female managers is more than that of female workers. In addition, the biggest gap in percentage between female employees and female employers can be seen in Sri Lanka.","words":226},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe diagram below shows the process for recycling cans.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The flow chart illustrates how aluminium cans are recycled.\n\nOverall, the whole procedure can be divided into7 stages, starting from throwing waste cans and ending at produce different beverage using recycled cans.\n\nAt the first step, people drop used cans into bins that are used only for collecting aluminium products. Subsequently, these cans are loaded in trucks and delivered to factories where contaminants are eliminated. Once the clean cans are separated from other waste they are shredded and then compressed into small pieces for further process.\n\nAt the following stage, these substances go through a heater where they are heated and melted into liquid. The next step in the process is pouring them in rolling molds to shape them into sheets with the thickness of 2.5mm to 6mm. After being molded into cans ready for recycling, they can be manufactured for the production of various beverage. Obviously, 74% of the cans are recyclable.","words":149},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe bar chart shows the percentage of males aged 16 to 25 who prefer watching or participating in different kinds of sports in a particular country.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The bar chart compares the proportion of 16 to 25 years old men in a certain nation who prefer to watch or participate in football, rugby, basketball, badminton, fishing, and horse riding and racing.\n\nAs seen in the chart, a higher percentage of young males tend to watch football, rugby, and horse racing matches rather than participate in them. Specifically, the highest percentages of those men watch football matches and horse racing (around 45% each), followed by that of those who like watching rugby (just above 30%). In contrast, the lowest percentage watches basketball, with around 11% doing so.\n\nRegarding the percentage of 16 to 25 years old men being involved in sports, almost half go fishing, three times more than the rate of those who prefer to watch that sport. Following fishing in terms of popularity is basketball, with 38% of young males playing it, and that percentage is almost 6% higher than that of those who merely play badminton. Notably, only approximately 11% of the young people play football, just one-fourth of the percentage of those watching it.\n\nOverall, rugby and badminton have minor disparities in terms of the percentage of males watching and participating in them, while the other four sports have significant differences in those aspects.","words":210},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe bar chart below shows the result of survey of patient who stayed in three different wards of a hospitals. It shows what percentage of the patients replied “very good” about various aspects of their hospital experience.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The bar chart compares the percentage of patients saying that the hospital staff, waiting time, quality of treatment, and quality of aftercare in Holly Ward, Maple Ward, and Oak Ward of a specific hospital are very good.\n\nAs seen, among the three wards, Holly Ward witnessed by far the highest percentage of patients giving “very good” comments for the four given hospital experience aspects. In Holly ward, 90% of patients claimed the quality of treatment is very good, followed by around 88% who thought the quality of aftercare is highly satisfactory. In contrast, just 75% of them felt very good about the waiting time, making up the lowest proportion among those surveyed.\n\nRegarding the survey data of the other two wards, those of Maple Ward were lower than those of Oak Ward, except for the feedback on waiting time, with the data on that aspect being same at about 53%. Specifically, the percentage of patients in Maple Ward stating the quality of aftercare was very good was 50%, while the corresponding statistics for Oak Ward was 70%. Meanwhile, the figure for quality of treatment in Oak Ward was 80%, 10% higher than that for Maple ward.\n\nOverall, among all aspects, quality of treatment received the highest percentage of patients rating it as very good, and Holly Ward ranked first in all aspects among the three wards.","words":226},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe table below shows the percentage of people living in different types of dwellings in three parts of Australia in 2001 and 2006.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"Types of dwelling\n\nVictoria\n\nThe Northern TerritoryTasmania\n\n2001\n\n2006\n\n2001\n\n2006\n\n2001\n\n2006\n\nHouse\n\n79\n\n76\n\n65\n\n63\n\n88\n\n89\n\nSemi-attached house\n\n11\n\n12\n\n13\n\n15\n\n3\n\n4\n\nFlat/Apartment\n\n9\n\n10\n\n8\n\n11\n\n7\n\n6\n\nother\n\n1\n\n2\n\n14\n\n11\n\n2\n\n1\n\nThe table illustrates the changes in the proportion of Australians residing in houses, semi-attached houses, apartments, and others in Victoria, the Northern Territory, and Tasmania in 2001 and 2006.\n\nAs seen in the table, the majority of residents in the three regions lived in houses, but the percentage of people doing so decreased slightly, except that in Tasmania. Specifically, just below 90% of people resided in houses in Tasmania, with a 1% increase to 89% noted in 2006. In contrast, the figure for Victoria and the Northern Territory witnessed a slight decrease from 79% to 76% and from 65% to 63%, respectively.\n\nWith regard to the percentages of people who dwelled in a semi-attached house, apartment, and other, they saw no more than 3% increases or decreases. Notably, in Victoria, the ratios of Victorians living in semi-attached houses and apartments grew to at least 10% each, and that of those staying in other house types climbed to 2%. In comparison, the statistics of Tasmanians living in apartments and other unnamed dwellings declined from 7% to 6% and from 2% to 1%, separately. Meanwhile, by far the highest percentage of people living in other types of houses was recorded in the Northern Territory, but it was reduced minimally from 14% to 11%.\n\nOverall, relatively higher percentages of people lived in houses in the three areas, and compared to the two states, the Northern Territory had more people living in other types of accommodation.","words":242},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe pie charts below show the coffee production, coffee consumption and the profit distribution around the world.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The pie charts illustrate the percentages of coffee consumed in Europe, America, Japan, and other regions of the world, the percentages of coffee produced in South Africa, Indonesia, Japan, and Vietnam, as well as how much profit is distributed to delivery, retailers, exporters, and producers.\n\nAs seen, the highest percentage of coffee is consumed by Europeans at 41%, 2% higher than that by Americans, while the Japanese drink the least proportion of coffee at 8%. Other parts of the world use up just 12% of it. Notably, Japan is the only coffee-consuming country that produces a significant amount of it, with 18% manufactured there, which is 2% more than the yield in Vietnam. Meanwhile, coffee production in South Africa is by far the largest, accounting for 44%, two times as much as that in Indonesia.\n\nAs for the profit distribution related to coffee, more than half of it (51%) is generated in delivery industries, followed by 24% in retail. However, as for coffee producers, they just receive 15% of the profits while exporters obtain the least at 10%.\n\nOverall, Europeans drink the highest percentage of coffee while South Africa is the main coffee producer. Additionally, the coffee delivery service is the most profitable\n\nbusiness among those compared.","words":207},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe table shows information about the number of Australian people who visited the seven most popular destinations in 1999 and 2009.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The table illustrates the changes in the quantity of Australians travelling to New Zealand, the United Kingdom, the United States, Indonesia, Fiji, Japan, and China in 1999 and 2009.\n\nAs seen in the table, the Australian tourist population to the seven most popular destinations all increased with different growth rates, and that to New Zealand, Indonesia, Fiji, Japan, and China grew by more than 100%. Specifically, the number of Australian visitors to New Zealand was by far the highest at 500,700 in 1999 and 1,064,00 in 2009, having a 113% increase. The data on visitors to Fiji experienced the same growth rate, rising from 120,000 to 257,000. Notably, although China received the highest growth rate of Australian guests at 249%, the actual statistics were relatively low (86,000 in 1999 and 300,000 in 2009).\n\nMeanwhile, despite the United Kingdom and the United States remaining the top three prevalent destinations, the data on Australian tourists there witnessed lower growth rates, with the UK attracting 640,000 Australian tourists in 2009, 60% more than it did in 1999. In contrast, the figure for the United States had the lowest growth rate at 33%, and the Australian tourist population there increased from 300,000 to 400,000.\n\nOverall, an increased number of Australians travelled abroad during the period, and the statistics of those who travelled to China surged more significantly than those to\n\nother nations.","words":228},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The pie charts below show the class sizes in primary schools in four states in Australia in 2010.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe pie charts compare the percentages of classrooms with more than 30 students, 26-30 students, 21-25 students, and 20 students or fewer in elementary schools in New South Wales (NSW), Western Australia (WA), Australian Capital Territory (ACT), and Southern Australia (SA) in 2010.\n\nAs seen, ACT and SA had the same pattern of class sizes. Specifically, classes having 21-25 students accounted for the highest percentages at 51% and 42%, respectively, followed by those with 26-30 students, registering at 35% and 32%, separately. The third most common class size in the two regions was 20 students or fewer, with 10% noted in ACT, twice as high as in SA. In contrast, the ratios of classes with more than 30 students were the lowest and approximately the same at 4%.\n\nMeanwhile, grouping 26-30 students in a class was dominant in NSW’s and WA’s primary schools (44% and 35%, respectively). However, the second most common class size in NSW was composed of 20 students or fewer (27%), while it was 21-25 students in WA (32%). Moreover, classes containing more than 30 students constituted the same percentage at 4% in the two areas.\n\nOverall, a class size of 30 students or more was the least typical, whereas a class enrolling 21-25 students was mainstream in the four Australian administrative\n\ndivisions.","words":216},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The diagrams below show the coastal village of Saville in 1980 and 2010.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe maps illustrate significant changes in the seaside town of Saville over a thirty-year period, from 1980 to 2010.\n\nIn 1980, Saville just had some holiday cottages and a hotel to accommodate tourists, and to the east of the cottages was a marsh. Moreover, Woodland was situated to the north of the marsh, separated from it by a road running from west to east, and that intersects with two parallel roads. Between that accommodation zone and the river located in the east of the village, a large space was not developed, and to the south of it was the beach which featured a tea room and sand dunes.\n\nIn 2010, the village experienced dramatic improvements. Specifically, the cottages were demolished, and the marsh was drained to construct permanent houses. To the north of those, the woodland was replaced by a golf course. Similarly, the tea room was redeveloped into a high-rise hotel and a restaurant. Furthermore, retirement villages were built to the west of the main road, with a boat club set up on the west bank of the river. Aside from those changes, holiday cottages had been constructed on the unspoiled east bank, with a new bridge and road constructed to access the cottages.\n\nOverall, the village developed substantially from a holiday settlement to a town with permanent houses, more hotels, and cottages built.","words":225},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The table below shows the number of temporary migrant workers in four countries in 2003 and 2006 and the number of these workers per 1000 people in these countries in 2006.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe table illustrates the changes in the number of temporary migrant workers in Australia, New Zealand, the United Kingdom, and the United States in 2003 and 2006, along with the number of these workers per 1,000 people in those countries in 2006.\n\nAs seen, the United States had the highest number of temporary migrant workers at 577,000, and the figure increased by 101,000 in 2006, which was equivalent to 2.3 migrant workers per 1,000 people. Similarly, the United Kingdom saw a significant increase in itinerant workers, whose number rose from 137,000 in 2003 to 266,000 in 2006, equating to 4.4 per 1,000 people, two times as much as that of the US.\n\nCompared to the relatively low rates of temporary workers in the US and the UK, those in New Zealand and Australia were much greater. Specifically, New Zealand had by far the highest worker-to-population ratio among the four countries at 21.1 in 2006, although the initial and eventual data were the lowest at 65,000 and 87,000, respectively. Meanwhile, Australia experienced an upturn in temporary migrant workers, whose statistics climbed from 152,000 in 2003 to 219,000 in 2006, accounting for the second highest worker-to-population ratio (10.7).\n\nOverall, while the United States had the largest total number of temporary migrant workers in both years, New Zealand had the highest proportion of seasonal workers\n\nrelative to its population in 2006.","words":228},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The graph below shows the percentage of Australian workforce in five industries between 1962 and 2012.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe graph illustrates the changes in the percentage of the Australian workforce employed in services, manufacturing, agriculture, construction, and mining from 1962 to 2012.\n\nAs seen, the services industry employed an increasingly higher percentage of the workforce, growing from just over half to approximately 78% in 2012, making it the dominant industry in terms of employment throughout the entire period. In contrast, mining consistently employed the least percentage of the workforce, starting at around 1% and rising slightly to 3% in 1992 before fluctuating minimally and ending at about 2%. Meanwhile, the employment rate in the construction sector remained relatively stable at around 10%.\n\nOn the other hand, the employment rates in manufacturing and agriculture witnessed continuous declines over the period. Specifically, the data for manufacturing was at almost 27% in 1960 but dropped to the same level as that for construction in 2012. Similarly, agriculture, which initially employed nearly 12% of the workforce, experienced a gradual decrease in the proportion of people it employed, falling below the construction employment rate after 1972 and reaching close to 3% by the end of the period.\n\nOverall, the services sector saw substantial growth in workforce employment from 1962 to 2012, while manufacturing and agriculture experienced a decline in the\n\nnumber of people they hired.","words":212},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The graph below shows the average house prices in three countries between 1997 and 2014.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe line graph illustrates the changes in the typical cost of houses in Country A, Country B, and Country C from 1997 to 2014.\n\nAs seen, the average house price of Country A was the highest over the given period, except from 1998 to 2000. Specifically, in 1997, the average house price in Country A was around 150,000 dollars, which had surged to its peak at approximately $530,000 by 2003. However, although it dropped to just above 440,000 dollars in 2006, the figure rebounded, reaching about 620,000 dollars in 2009 and stabilizing in the five years that followed.\n\nAs for Country B’s usual pricing of houses, despite being slightly higher than Country A’s from 1998 to 2000, it was by far lower in other years. Notably, the prices started at roughly 130,000 dollars in 1997 and increased steadily, reaching about 375,000 dollars in 2005, after which it witnessed a minor dip to 280,000 dollars in 2008. After that, the prices rose again and reached just above 500,000 dollars in 2012 before decreasing slightly to just below that price. Meanwhile, Country C had the lowest house prices among the three countries, although its prices experienced a generally linear growth from around 120,000 dollars in 1997 to almost 280,000 dollars at the end of the period.\n\nOverall, Country A and B had relatively higher and volatile house prices during the period, while Country C saw more moderate changes in its real estate costs.","words":241},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The graph below shows the percentage of four different age groups visiting a cinema once a month or more in Britain between 1984 and 2003.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe graph illustrates the changes in the percentage of people aged 7-14, 15-24, 25-34, and 35 and over visiting the cinema at least once a month in Britain from 1984 to 2003.\n\nAs seen, the cinema attendance rate of people below 35 years old generally increased with significant fluctuations, along with that of the 15-24 age group, which was the highest during the period. Notably, 16% of the 15-24 age group went to the cinema in 1984, and that grew steadily to approximately 32% in 1992. After some fluctuating from around 32% to 50%, attendance rate peaked at 58% in 2002 before dropping to 52% in 2003. Although the 7-14 and 25-34 age groups started at 10% and around 4%, respectively, they experienced similar variation patterns and both ended at 30%, with\n\nthe former’s attendance rate relatively higher than the latter’s, except in 2001.\n\nIn contrast, while the 35 and over age group showed a steady upward trend in movie theatre attendance, it remained the lowest throughout the period. Specifically, the attendance rate was about 2% in 1984 and rose minimally to 3% in 1986 before dropping back to its initial level in 1988. Despite this fluctuation, it continued to climb steadily, reaching approximately 13% by the end of the period.\n\nOverall, cinema attendance went up across all age groups during the period, with the 15-24 age group consistently having the largest percentage of monthly cinema-goers,\n\nwhile the 35 and over age group had the smallest attendance rate.","words":252},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The bar chart below gives information about the percentage of the population living in urban areas in the world and in different continents. Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"Write at least 150 words.\n\nThe bar chart illustrates the changes in the percentage of people living in towns and cities in 1950 and 2000, as well as the projected figure for 2030, for Africa, Asia, Latin America/Caribbean, and the whole world.\n\nAs seen, in 1950, approximately 30% of the world's population lived in urban areas. By 2000, that figure had risen to around 48%, and it is projected to reach 60% in 2030. This global trend is mirrored in the regional data, albeit with different rates of variation.\n\nNotably, Latin America and the Caribbean have the highest levels of urbanization over the given period. Specifically, in 1950, the urban population there was just over 40%, but it increased to 70% by 2000, with an expected rise to nearly 80% by 2030. Meanwhile, although Africa showed significant growth, its population residing in cities and towns was the lowest, climbing from about 12% in 1950 to around 38% in 2000, and that statistic is expected to exceed 50% by 2030. Similarly, Asia's urban population rate tripled from approximately 13% initially to the same rate as Africa’s in 2000, with an estimated increase to nearly 56% by 2030.\n\nOverall, the percentage of the urban population in Latin America and the Caribbean was consistently the largest during the observed period. Additionally, there was a substantial and ongoing shift towards urban living globally and across all examined\n\nregions.","words":229},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The chart below gives information about the world’s top three producers of four different dairy products (milk, cheese, butter, milk powder) in 2012.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe charts compare the average intake of protein (both animal and other sources) and calories among people in India, East Africa, Latin America, and North America.\n\nAs observed, animal protein consumption is generally higher than other protein sources in most regions, except in India. Notably, only North Americans exceed the ideal protein level of 66 units, consuming a total of 80 units (62 units from animal sources and 18 units from other protein sources). In Latin America, the total protein intake is significantly lower at 45 units, with 32 units coming from animal sources. In East Africa, the intake of animal and other proteins is almost equal, at 16 units and 15 units, respectively. A similar pattern is seen in India, though their animal protein consumption is even lower at just 13 units.\n\nRegarding average calorie intake, North America records the highest at around 3,600 calories, 700 calories above the ideal level. In the other regions, calorie intake falls short of the standard, particularly in India, where the average intake is 900 calories below the ideal. East Africans also experience a calorie deficit, having only 2,350 calories on average, whereas Latin Americans fare better with an intake of 2,800 calories, which is close to the ideal.\n\nOverall, there is a significant disparity in nutritional intake across these regions, with North America far exceeding both the ideal protein and calorie levels, while India and\n\nEast Africa lag behind, particularly in terms of calorie consumption.","words":243},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The bar graph illustrates the number of trips made by children in a particular country in 1990 and 2010 using different modes of transport.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe bar chart illustrates the changes in the popularity of five different means of transport among the 5-12s in two separate years: 1990 and 2010.\n\nOverall, children in this age group had travelled less in 2010, with car travelling dominated the trips.\n\nIn 1990, walking was the most common way to go to school, with more than 12 millions trips being made by children aged 5-12. Conversely, car travel, with roughly\n\n4.3 million trips, was the least popular commuting way for children. Additionally, the number of bus trips were much higher at around 7 million, just ahead of cycling trips as well as walking and bus trips.\n\nHowever, in 2010, the level of trips made by all modes of transport showed a downward trend, except for car trips which recorded a significant growth to almost 12 million, becoming the most popular way to travel to school. On the contrary, walking experienced the most considerate drop, halving to 6 million trips, while trips by bus, walking and bus, cycling had decreased to 5 million, 3.5 million and only 2 million\n\nrespectively.","words":180},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The pie charts below show the percentage of students on the one adult education center taking a various course offered in 1985 and this year.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe pie charts depict the percentage distribution of students enrolled in Fitness and Dance, Art, Cookery, Music Appreciation, Local History, Modern Languages and IT at an adult education center in 1985 and the current year.\n\nIn 1985, modern languages was the most popular course, attracting 24% of the students. Fitness and Dance followed closely with 22% enrolling in it, while art courses accounted for 17% of enrollments. On the other hand, local history and cookery both had a significant and similar share of students at 15% and 14% respectively. In contrast, music appreciation drew only 8%% of the students.\n\nIn the present year, the distribution of enrollments has witnessed notable changes. While fitness and dance continue to maintain their appeal, attracting 22%% of enrollees, modern languages has declined in popularity, as only 17% took it. Art courses have also experienced a slight decrease in enrollment, now representing 15% of students. However, Cookery has become more popular, with its share rising to 19%. A significant shift is observed with the introduction of IT courses which now represent 27% of total enrollments, and with the removal of music appreciation from the course offerings.\n\nOverall, a marked change in student preferences is observed over time with an increase in enrollment in IT courses, while traditional subjects like modern languages have seen a decline in popularity, and the consistency in the preference for fitness and\n\ndance courses is also notable.","words":236},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The graph below shows the change in percentages of staffs who applied the day off for more than 5 days in five different countries from 1991 and 2001.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe graph below illustrates the variation in the percentage of employees requesting more than five days off across five different countries between 1991 and 2001.\n\nOverall, the UK and Germany experienced a moderate increase in the proportion of people opting for extended leave, while France, the Netherlands, and Sweden saw a general decline in this period. The UK consistently had the highest percentage of people taking more than five days off, whereas the other three countries had comparatively lower figures.\n\nStarting at 4%, this figure in the UK decreased to 3% in 1995, before rising noticeably to reach a high of 5% in 2001. Germany followed a similar trend. Having remained at 3% in the first three years, the level of working staff having day off more than 5 days dipped in 1995, followed by a noticeable increase to around 4.5% by the end of this period.\n\nBy contrast, the percentages for the remaining three countries were notably lower. France saw erratic changes in the levels of employees taking extended holidays throughout this decade, whereas both Netherlands and Sweden exhibited similar trends, growing from around 1.5% to reach a peak of almost 2% during the first half\n\nof the period, and then both declined back to their initial levels by 2001.","words":211},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The plans below show the ground floor of a library in 2001 and how it was redeveloped in 2009.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe maps compare the changes which had taken place on the ground floor of a library between 2001 and 2009.\n\nOverall, there had been a shift towards becoming a more modernized and more child-friendly library over this period.\n\nThe entrance in the south and the librarian’s desk next to it had remained unchanged. In 2001, the southeast corner used to be an enclosed area for stairs and children’s books, whereas this area had turned into an open space with computers and the size of the staircase had been reduced by 2009. The section for newspapers and magazines, previously located in the east of the library, had been redeveloped into the place where visitors could watch and rent films and DVDs. There were a lot of tables in the middle of the library, but the number of tables had been reduced in order to make way for a children’s area.\n\nIn 2001, the north part of the library contained books on travel, cooking, and videos, while the history section occupied the northwest corner and was twice the size of the other three sections. By 2009, a notable change has been the conversion of this area into a children’s book section. The western part of the library was originally designed for fiction, but by 2009, its size had been reduced to one-fifth of its original area. The remaining space was divided into four equally-sized sections for travel, history,\n\ncooking and self-help books.","words":240},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The table below gives information about populations in Australia and Malaysia from 1980 to 2002.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe table compares six demographic indicators between Australia and Malaysia in 1980 and 2002.\n\nOverall, while the population size in both countries increased considerably over the period, Malaysia’s population grew at a significantly faster rate but maintained a younger demographic profile. Notably, gender distribution remained balanced in both nations throughout the years.\n\nIn terms of total population, both countries witnessed a growth, with Malaysia’s population nearly doubling to 24.3 million, whereas Australia’s population increased more modestly from 14.7 to 19.6 million. The gender ratio remained consistently balanced at approximately 50:50 in both years for each country.\n\nIn 1980, Malaysia had a noticeably higher birth rate, almost doubling that of Australia at 3.2%. However, both countries experienced a decline in this indicator, with Malaysia’s rate dropping by 1% and Australia’s by 0.2% by 2002.\n\nBy contrast, the annual population growth indicators were less dramatic, standing at 2.2% for Malaysia and 1.2% for Australia. The aging population showed a different pattern. There was a substantial growth in senior population in Australia, rising from\n\n9.6 to 12.4 million, while the level of seniors in Malaysia only grew 0.6 to 4.3 million.","words":188},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The diagrams show the structure of solar panel and their use.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe given diagrams illustrate the structure and function of a basic solar panel system, particularly how it is used to heat air and water.\n\nAs seen in the first diagram, the basic structure of the solar panel consists of a simple box with a transparent top and a dark sunlight-absorbing bottom layer. The transparent cover allows sunlight to enter the box, where heat energy is then trapped.\n\nThe second diagram demonstrates how the solar panel heats air. Specifically, the sun rays are absorbed in the system, and when cool air enters the box through an inlet, it is warmed by the heat energy from the sun. Afterwards, the warm air exits through an outlet, providing heated air for various purposes.\n\nIn the third diagram, a U-shaped water pipe is employed to connect the inlet and outlet, functioning as a heat exchanger. As cool water flows through the pipe inside the box and the sunlight heats the box, the water in the pipe is warmed up and subsequently flows out through the outlet as hot water.\n\nOverall, the solar panel design is simple and efficient, an eco-friendly way to heat both air and water by trapping and converting heat energy that can be accessed and\n\nreleased through an outlet.","words":208},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The diagram below shows the process of growing and preparing pineapples and pineapple products.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe flow chart gives information about how to cultivate pineapples and the steps involved in manufacturing pineapple commodities.\n\nOverall, the pineapple cultivation takes a year, and various sizes of pineapples are processed into three categories of products.\n\nFirstly, the pineapple crowns are planted in the ground, with their crowns spaced 26 centimeters apart in a climate where temperatures range from 23 ℃ to 30 ℃. After\n\nseven months of growth, pineapples are treated with ethaline to stimulate further growth for another 5 months until they are ready for harvesting. At this point, the plants almost reach a height of 30 centimeters and weigh around 2 kilograms.\n\nThe phase of producing pineapple products begins with a cleaning process, and then pineapples are divided into three groups: the smallest, medium-sized and largest ones. The largest pineapples undergo a simple wax coating before being placed in crates and then shipped by freights to other countries. In contrast, the other two groups have their tops cut off and rinds peeled. The smallest pineapples are put into the extractor\n\nand turned into pineapple juice, while the larger ones are sliced and canned.","words":188},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The graph shows the percentage of families owning different number of computers in a European country from 1997 to 2011.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe given graph illustrates the changes in computer ownership among households in a European country from 1997 to 2011, showing the percentages of households with no computer, one computer, two computers and three or more computers.\n\nAs shown, the percentages of homes with two computers and those with three or more computers grew significantly over the 14-year period. In 1997, ownership of two computers stood at about 6%, and it rose gradually, reaching nearly 28% in 2011 and surpassing the proportion of families without a computer. Similarly, although families that owned three or more computers were nearly nonexistent in the first two years, their percentage climbed steadily and peaked at just above 7% by the end of the period.\n\nIn contrast, despite the households having no computer accounting for the largest proportion at the beginning (around 48%), their data declined continuously over the years, dropping sharply to approximately 10% in 2011. The statistics on those with a single computer, however, showed little variation, remaining relatively steady at around 45% throughout the period.\n\nOverall, a marked shift towards increased computer ownership was witnessed with a noticeable rise in households possessing multiple computers and a corresponding\n\ndecline in those without any computers.","words":200},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The graph below shows changes in average monthly cost (euros)of four kinds of mobile contracts in a particular country in 2012.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe graph illustrates the changes in the typical price of a contract per month for Domo, Lex, Sim TX, and Alpha cellphones in a particular European country in the period from January to September 2002.\n\nAs seen, the expenditures for Domo, Lex, and Alpha showed general upward trends, despite having some fluctuations, In January, Domo's monthly cost stood at approximately €15 and gradually rose in the succeeding months. By July, it had peaked at around €25 before it slightly dipped to €23 in September. Similarly, Lex's monthly payment plan began at 12 but fell slightly to 10 in February. However, it then experienced a gradual increase, reaching €l8 by September. In contrast, Alpha's contract price remained the lowest during the first half of 2002, but it surged and became the highest at €25 in September.\n\nOn the other hand, Sim TX followed a different pattern. Starting at around €l0 in January, its costs dropped by €l before February, then rose slightly to just below €13 in June, with the second-largest monthly cost recorded (almost l and 9 euros more than Lex's and Alpha's, respectively). Despite that, after June, Sim TX's price sharply declined, falling below those of Alpha in July and arriving at the lowest monthly cost of approximately €7 in September.\n\nOverall, Domo maintained the greatest average monthly cost for most of the period except in September. Meanwhile, Alpha, which had the lowest contract rate in the\n\nearly months, saw a sharp upswing in its data toward the end.","words":250},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The graph below shows the number of enquiries to tourist information office made by telephone, letter/email, and in person from January 2001 to June 2001.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\nThe graph illustrates changes in the volume of inquiries handled by a tourist information office in a particular city through in-person visits, letters/emails, and telephone calls during the first half of 2011.\n\nAs seen, enquiries made by telephone and in person both increased considerably in general. Notably, the former started at 900 in January, accounting for the highest number among the three methods in that month, and it experienced slight fluctuations in the first three months before rising steadily to 1,600 in June. Meanwhile, despite the latter being by far the lowest (around 400), the figure for it grew steadily each month, surpassing that for letter or email enquiries and telephone enquiries in March and April, respectively. By June, in-person enquiries reached approximately 1,900, the highest volume recorded.\n\nOn the other hand, queries made by letter/email registered at around 750 in January and decreased gradually throughout the six months, dropping to just under 400 by May and stabilizing onwards. This method became the least preferred after March, coinciding with the rising popularity of in-person and telephone communication.\n\nOverall, questions made in person and by telephone surged over the period, while those made by letter or email saw a steady decline. Among the three methods, in.person enquiries experienced the most rapid growth, eventually becoming the most\n\ncommon means of communication.","words":219},{"date":"2月24日","q":"The chart below shows the average price per kilo of clothing imported into the European Union from seven major countries of origin in 1997 and 2003.","essay":"Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.\n\n【Sample】\n\nThe bar chart illustrates the average price per kilogram of clothing imported into the European Union (EU) from seven major countries-Tunisia, Morocco, Turkey, India, Indonesia, China, and Bangladesh-in the years 1997 and 2003.\n\nIn 1997, Tunisia had an average price of around 15 euros per kilo, which significantly increased to around 25 euros in 2003, making it the highest price recorded in the chart. Similarly, prices for Moroccan and Turkish imports showed slight growth, both starting at around 15 euros per kilo in 1997 and increasing marginally to approximately 18 and 19 euros, respectively, by 2003.\n\nIndia and Indonesia, which had mid-range import costs, saw contrasting trends. While India's clothing prices rose from around 10 euros in 1997 to over 15 euros in 2003 Indonesia's prices experienced a slight decline, from nearly 10 euros to around 7 euros per kilogram. Meanwhile, China's exports increased slightly in price, rising from about7 euros in 1997 to around 9 euros in 2003.\n\nFinally, Bangladesh consistently had the lowest prices among the seven countries, with prices hovering around 5 euros per kilogram in both 1997 and 2003, showing little to no variation over the period.\n\nOverall, there was a general trend of increased prices in 2003 compared to 1997, with the exception of a few countries, such as Bangladesh, which experienced minimal or no significant change. Tunisia and Morocco consistently showed the highest prices for clothing exports to the EU in both years, while Bangladesh remained the lowest-\n\npriced exporter.","words":245},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe bar chart below shows the percentage of share of total world production in Asia, Europe, and other parts of the world from the years 1840 to 2000.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The bar chart illustrates changes in the share of global production in Asia, Europe, and the rest of the world from 1840 to 2000.\n\nAs seen, Europe and other regions outside Asia followed a bell-shaped trend in their global production shares. In 1840, Europe contributed two-fifths to the world production. After that year, its share rose significantly to a peak of 48% but then declined steadily, reaching its lowest point at approximately 27.5% by 2000. Similarly, the rest of the world, which initially accounted for just 9%, saw a substantial increase in their collective output, which peaked at 37% in 1960. However, that figure dipped slightly to 33% by the end of the period.\n\nIn contrast, Asia witnessed a U-shaped trend, with its share of the total world production going through a sharp drop followed by a robust recovery. At the start of the period, Asia held the largest share of the global production, at just over 50%. This dominance diminished as its share fell sharply, bottoming out at 20% between 1880 and 1920. After 1920, however, the figure rebounded significantly, climbing to 39% by 2000 when Asia reasserted its position as the leading contributor.\n\nOverall, the global production shares of Asia and Europe reversed trends over the period, with Asia regaining its dominance in 2000. Meanwhile, the production share of the rest of the world saw a consistent uptrend over the period, with only a marginal\n\ndownturn observed toward the end.","words":242},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe table below shows employment patterns for males and females in New Zealand in 1993 and 2003.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The table illustrates changes in the employment rates of males and females in manufacturing, building, commerce, services, IT, and farming in New Zealand between 1993 and 2003.\n\nAs seen, the proportion of males employed in IT was the highest across all sectors, despite it decreasing from 80% in 1993 to 70% in 2003. In contrast, the percentage of females in IT rose significantly from 20% to 30%. A similar trend was observed in farming, where male employment declined from 75% to 65%, while the female workforce increased from 25% to 35%. Manufacturing and building followed a comparable pattern, with male employment dropping slightly from 55% to 52% and female participation increasing from 45% to 48%. In building, the proportion of men fell from 68% to 60%, whereas that of women experienced growth from 32% to 40%.\n\nOn the other hand, in services, women dominated in 1993, making up 85% of the workforce. However, that figure dropped significantly to 60% in 2003. Conversely, male employment in that sector rose sharply from 15% to 40%. Commerce saw a similar shift, with the data on males climbing from 45% to 50%, while the proportion of females slid from 55% to the same level as that of men in 2003.\n\nOverall, although male employment rates generally declined over the decade, they remained higher in most sectors. Moreover, the rate of employed females increased in several fields, particularly IT and farming, but their dominance in services diminished\n\nsignificantly.","words":243},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe graph below shows four methods of dealing with waste in four countries.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The bar chart illustrates the percentage of waste processed through various methods, namely burning, chemical use, recycling, and landfilling, in the Netherlands, Italy, Spain, and the UK.\n\nAs seen, recycling is the dominant method in the Netherlands, accounting for nearly 68% of the total waste treatment. The remaining three methods are significantly less utilized, with the amount of waste disposed through them ranging around 10%. Among those, landfilling represents the smallest proportion (8%).\n\nIn contrast, Italy and Spain adopt a more evenly distributed approach to waste management. Landfilling emerges as the most common method in both countries where 40% of waste is buried. Burning is the second most prominent method, but the figure is higher in Spain, at 30%, compared to the 21% in Italy, whereas the same proportion of waste, 18%, is processed chemically. Recycling, however, plays a relatively minor role, especially in Spain, where it accounts for just 12%.The UK relies overwhelmingly on landfilling, with almost 67% of waste managed through this method, the highest proportion in the four countries. Both chemical use and recycling contribute approximately 15% each, while burning is negligible, used for just 4% of the nation’s waste.\n\nOverall, landfilling is the primary waste disposal method in Italy, Spain, and the UK, whereas recycling predominates in the Netherlands. Moreover, among the countries,\n\nItaly demonstrates the most balanced use of the four methods.","words":227},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe bar chart below gives information on the numbers of workers in different occupations who migrated to Australia between 2009 and 2012.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The bar chart illustrates how many skilled accountants, cooks, software engineers, and computer programmers migrated to Australia from 2009 to 2012.\n\nAs seen, accountants consistently had the highest number among skilled migrants throughout the period. Starting at 20,000 in 2009, the figure peaked at 27,500 in 2010 before dropping sharply to 21,000 by 2012. A similar trend was observed among software engineers, whose number initially stood at around 19,000 in 2009. After that, the total gradually increased, reaching a high of 22,000 in 2011, but it declined significantly to approximately 17,000 in 2012.\n\nThe corresponding statistics on cooks and computer programmers followed different patterns, with cooks far outnumbering programmers. Specifically, the number of cooks migrating to Australia slid to a low of 15,000 in 2010 but rebounded to 18,000 in 2011 and then fell slightly to 16,000 in 2012. Conversely, the number of computer programmers showed a more erratic trend, as it doubled to 4,000 in 2010, dipped back to its initial level in 2011 (2,000), and then surged to 5,000 in 2012, showing the most consistent growth among the data on the four occupations.\n\nOverall, accountants remained the largest group of skilled migrants in all four years, while computer programmers consistently accounted for the smallest share. Meanwhile, although the figures for most occupations experienced fluctuations, a general decline by 2012 was evident in all categories except computer programmers.","words":229},{"date":"2月24日","q":"You should spend about 20 minutes on this task.\nThe graph shows the percentage of different age groups of cinema visitors in a particular country from 1978 to 2008.\nSummarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\nWrite at least 150 words.","essay":"The graph illustrates the percentage of moviegoers across four age groups, namely 14-24, 25-34, 35-49, and older 50, in a certain country during the 30-year period from 1978 to 2008.\n\nAs seen, younger age groups (14-24 and 25-34) consistently showed higher participation rate in cinema visits compared to older age groups, with the 14-24 age group leading throughout the period. In 1978, this age group recorded the largest percentage of cinema-goers (90%), followed by the 25-34 group (around 80%). In 1988, the figures for both groups experienced declines to approximately 70% and 61%, respectively. However, from 1988 onwards, attendance rate among these groups rebounded and peaked in 1998 before gradually decreasing to around 80% and 65% by 2008.\n\nMeanwhile, cinema attendance among the 35-49 and 50+ age groups was significantly lower. Specifically, roughly 60% and 40% of those groups, respectively, went to the movies in 1978, but their proportions slid by about 20% in 1988. However, their attendance levels had returned to their initial values by 1998. After that, the data on the 35-49 age group experienced a steady downturn, ending at 50% in 2008, while that on the 50+ group witnessed the opposite trend, increasing to match that on the 35-49 group in 2008, although it consistently remained the lowest.\n\nOverall, while younger age groups dominated cinema attendance throughout the period, the statistics on older age groups saw a gradual upward trajectory, narrowing\n\nthe gap during the 30 years.","words":240}];
